    </note>
    <p>When using the order of operations to simplify an expression that has square roots, we treat the radical as a grouping symbol.</p>
    <example number="9.3">
      <problem>
        <p>Simplify: ⓐ $$\sqrt{25} + \sqrt{144}$$ ⓑ $$\sqrt{25 + 144}$$.</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <p>ⓐ</p>
        <table summary="Illustrates the step-by-step simplification of a mathematical expression involving square roots using the order of operations.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\sqrt{25} + \sqrt{144}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Use the order of operations.</cell>
              <cell>$$5 + 12$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>17</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>ⓑ</p>
        <table summary="Steps illustrating the simplification of a mathematical square root expression.">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\sqrt{25 + 144}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify under the radical sign.</cell>
              <cell>$$\sqrt{169}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>13</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Notice the different answers in parts ⓐ and ⓑ !</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
